## Exact statements derived

1. For prime ell > H"'^2*2, primitive signed weights give distinct forbidden residues A/B mod ell: equality implies ell | AD-BC with |AD-BC| <= 2H''^2; the primitive representatives with positive denominator coincide. Their number is W(H)=4 sum_{b<=H} phi(b)-2 ~ (12/pi''^2) H''^2. Thus conditional on p,q nonzero mod ell the excluded fraction is W(H)/(ell-1), not W(H)/ell exactly.

2. The direct CRT construction works for any fixed finite band once W(H)<ell-1 for every ell. Choose an allowed ratio at each ell, and prime p,q in the resulting residue classes modulo Q=product ell. PNT in arithmetic progressions, with Q held fixed, supplies balanced pairs at arbitrarily large height. It does NOT establish the desired relation P~Y''^3 H: Q=exp(Theta(Y)) for a full dyadic band (also for primes 3 mod 4 in (Y,16Y]).

3. Composite modulus caution. For m=4k, ratio r=2k-1 is a unit. If A=rB mod m is primitive, B is odd (otherwise A is even), so A+B=2k mod 4k. Consequently max(|A|,B)>=k. There is a nonprimitive vector (-2,2) of norm 2. For m=12, r=5, no primitive vector has norm <=3; the primitive minimum is 5. Thus an unqualified primitive sqrt(m) Minkowski guarantee for composites is false.

4. Literal positive guarantees outside the useful parameter range exist. All integers in (2P,4P] cover every balanced pair with H=1, since p+q lies in that band and is coprime to N. J~Y, so this is a c=1/2 statement with huge cost. Also the exact-zero vector (A,B)=(p,q) covers every modulus when H>=q. These do not address Y~P''^{1/3} or a factoring exponent improvement.

5. CRT is an obstruction for statements uniform in unrestricted height. It does not rule out the polynomial-height coverage problem.

