# Independent analysis before literature search (18 October 2026)

## Exact statements derived

1. For prime ell > H^2*2, primitive signed weights give distinct forbidden residues A/B mod ell: equality implies ell | AD-BC with |AD-BC| <= 2H^2; the primitive representatives with positive denominator coincide. Their number is W(H)=4 sum_{b<=H} phi(b)-2 ~ (12/pi^2) H^2. Thus conditional on p,q nonzero mod ell the excluded fraction is W(H)/(ell-1), not W(H)/ell exactly.

2. The direct CRT construction works for any fixed finite band once W(H)<ell-1 for every ell. Choose an allowed ratio at each ell, and prime p,q in the resulting residue classes modulo Q=product ell. PNT in arithmetic progressions, with Q held fixed, supplies balanced pairs at arbitrarily large height. It does NOT establish the desired relation P~Y^3 H: Q=exp(Theta(Y)) for a full dyadic band (also for primes 3 mod 4 in (Y,16Y]).

3. Composite modulus caution. For m=4k, ratio r=2k-1 is a unit. If A=rB mod m is primitive, B is odd (otherwise A is even), so A+B=2k mod 4k. Consequently max(|A|,B)>=k. There is a nonprimitive vector (-2,2) of norm 2. For m=12, r=5, no primitive vector has norm <=3; the primitive minimum is 5. Thus an unqualified primitive sqrt(m) Minkowski guarantee for composites is false.

4. Literal positive guarantees outside the useful parameter range exist. All integers in (2P,4P] cover every balanced pair with H=1, since p+q lies in that band and is coprime to N. J~Y, so this is a c=1/2 statement with huge cost. Also the exact-zero vector (A,B)=(p,q) covers every modulus when H>=q. These do not address Y~P^{1/3} or a factoring exponent improvement.

5. CRT is an obstruction for statements uniform in unrestricted height. It does not rule out the polynomial-height coverage problem.

6. Write H=Y^{1/2} J^{-c}, J=Y^j, 0<=j<=1. Equating P*Y^{-3/2-cj} and Y^{1+j(1-2c)} gives N exponent [1+j(1-2c)]/[5+2j(1-3c)]. Its derivative in j has sign 3-4c. Thus the optimum is j=0 unless c>3/4? CHECK ALGEBRA: window is H P/Y^2 =P Y^{-3/2-cj}; target=JH^2=Y^{1+j(1-2c)}; exponent=N^{ (1+j(1-2c))/(5+2j(1-3c)) }. derivative numerator 5(1-2c)-2(1-3c)=3-4c, threshold c=3/4. This differs from user formula! Need examine cost definition and optimization: for j=1 H=Y^{1/2-c}, target=Y^{2-2c}; balance P=Y^{7/2-c}; X=N^{(1-c)/(7/2-c)}. Compare 1/5: 5-5c<3.5-c iff c>3/8. In expression above denominator should be 5+2j(1-3c)? balance exponent (1+j-2cj)+(3/2+cj)=5/2+j(1-c). Thus denominator 5+2j(1-c). derivative=5(1-2c)-2(1-c)=3-8c; threshold3/8. Correct formula: E(j,c)=[1+j(1-2c)]/[5+2j(1-c)].

7. A theorem for all short intervals does not directly apply to the thin rank-two set of residuals. In particular calling those formulations equivalent without a separate embedding argument is invalid. The band-prime-factor question is exactly a maximal-gap question for the union of multiples, but restricting to prime p,q is a further specialization.

## Unresolved before search

No selective improvement in the useful range proved yet. Composite divisors in ordinary intervals may be accessible to reciprocal exponential sums: standard exponent pair (1/6,2/3) in sum exp(hx/m), m~x^{1/3}, together with a truncated sawtooth expansion gives error x^{2/7} (logs), hence existence of a divisor in that band in intervals of length x^{2/7+epsilon}. This needs careful checking and literature confirmation. Such a theorem alone does not handle residual GAPs.

The negative polylog statement needs a lower bound for prime pairs avoiding roughly H^2 rational residue classes at every band prime of size P^{1/3}. Direct CRT with all constraints has exponential modulus. Standard fixed-depth sieve information is not a proof when H^2/log P diverges. No rigorous implication to twin primes or another named open problem has been derived.

